from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Astrophysics mainly studies...","options":["the physics of stars, galaxies, and the universe","the chemistry of rocks found on Earth","the biology of deep-ocean life","the history of ancient trade maps"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Astrophysics applies the laws of physics to celestial objects and the universe as a whole."},{"prompt":"Visible light is one part of...","options":["the electromagnetic spectrum","the periodic table of elements","the Earth''s water cycle","the carbon cycle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Visible light is a thin band within the full electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes radio, infrared, ultraviolet, X-ray, and gamma light."},{"prompt":"When a light source moves away from us, its light is...","options":["blueshifted","redshifted","unchanged in wavelength","absorbed completely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Motion away stretches the waves to longer wavelengths, a redshift."},{"prompt":"Stars generate their energy mainly by...","options":["chemical burning like a fire","nuclear fusion in the core","friction between gas layers","reflecting sunlight"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fusion of light nuclei in the core releases the energy that makes a star shine."},{"prompt":"The Sun is a star located in which galaxy?","options":["the Andromeda galaxy","the Milky Way","the Large Magellanic Cloud","the Triangulum galaxy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Sun sits in the disk of our home galaxy, the Milky Way."},{"prompt":"Hubble''s law says that more distant galaxies...","options":["move toward us faster","move away from us faster","stay perfectly still","all move at the same speed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recession speed is proportional to distance, so farther galaxies recede faster."},{"prompt":"A black hole is a region where...","options":["gravity is so strong that not even light escapes","there is no matter at all","a star has simply stopped spinning","cold dust has collected"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inside the event horizon the escape speed exceeds the speed of light, so nothing escapes."},{"prompt":"Dark matter is inferred mainly because...","options":["galaxies rotate faster than visible matter can explain","the night sky is dark","stars appear to twinkle","comets grow bright tails"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The extra gravity needed to hold fast-rotating galaxies together points to unseen mass."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What astrophysics studies and how we observe the universe', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain in plain terms what astrophysics is, list the main bands of the electromagnetic spectrum from radio waves through gamma rays, and say why astronomers build different telescopes to catch different kinds of light. You will also be able to describe the difference between an optical telescope on a mountain and a space telescope above the atmosphere. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, you saw that visible light is only one slice of a much wider spectrum. Before reading on, try to name one kind of light your eyes cannot see but a telescope can. Hint: your television remote and a hospital bone scan both use invisible light. By the end of this lesson you should be comfortable placing radio, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, and gamma-ray light in order of wavelength, and matching each band to the kind of object it reveals best, from cold gas clouds to violently exploding stars."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Astrophysics is the branch of physics that studies the nature of stars, planets, galaxies, and the universe as a whole. While classical astronomy records where objects are and how they move, astrophysics asks what they are made of, how hot they are, how they produce energy, and how they change over billions of years. Almost everything we know about the distant universe arrives as light, so understanding light is the heart of the subject. Light is an electromagnetic wave, and the full range of possible wavelengths is called the electromagnetic spectrum. From the longest wavelength to the shortest, the main bands are radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, and gamma ray. Visible light is the thin band our eyes evolved to detect. Earth''s atmosphere blocks much of the rest, which is why some telescopes sit on high, dry mountains and others orbit in space. Each band carries different information, so astronomers combine many views of the same object."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"A telescope does two jobs: it gathers light over a large area and it focuses that light to form an image. A bigger mirror collects more light, so it can see fainter and more distant objects. Radio telescopes use huge metal dishes because radio wavelengths are long; a single dish can span hundreds of feet. Optical telescopes use polished mirrors, and the largest ones are eight to ten meters across. Space telescopes avoid the blurring and absorption of the atmosphere. The Hubble Space Telescope works mainly in visible and ultraviolet light, while the James Webb Space Telescope is tuned for infrared, which lets it see cool dust and very distant, redshifted galaxies. X-ray and gamma-ray telescopes must fly above the atmosphere because that light never reaches the ground. As a rule of thumb, cool objects glow in infrared and radio, warm objects in visible light, and extremely hot or violent events, such as gas falling onto a black hole, shine in X-rays and gamma rays."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Put these in order of wavelength, longest first: X-ray, radio, visible, infrared. 2) Why must an X-ray telescope be placed in space? 3) If you wanted to study cold dust in a star-forming cloud, which band would you choose? 4) Does a larger telescope mirror help you see fainter objects, and why? 5) Name one reason to put a visible-light telescope on a high mountain. Answers: 1) radio, infrared, visible, X-ray. 2) Earth''s atmosphere absorbs X-rays, so they never reach the ground; only an orbiting telescope can detect them. 3) Infrared, because cool dust glows most strongly at infrared wavelengths. 4) Yes - a larger mirror gathers more light each second, so a faint source builds up a detectable signal. 5) High mountains have thinner, drier, steadier air above them, which reduces absorption and blurring and gives sharper images."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking visible light is the only or the most important kind of light in astronomy - in fact each band reveals objects the others miss, and much of modern discovery comes from radio, infrared, and X-ray views. Mistake: assuming a telescope mainly magnifies, when its first job is to gather light; magnification without enough light just gives a bigger, fainter blur. Mistake: believing every telescope can sit on the ground, when some kinds of light are blocked by the atmosphere and demand space observatories. Exit ticket: (a) What does astrophysics study that pure position-tracking astronomy does not emphasize? (b) Order these from longest to shortest wavelength: gamma ray, radio, visible. Answers: (a) the physical nature of objects - their composition, temperature, energy source, and evolution - using the laws of physics; (b) radio, visible, gamma ray."}]}'::jsonb, 'Astronomy observes and catalogs what is in the sky; astrophysics asks why it behaves that way, using physics. The two words overlap in everyday use.', 13),
  (3, 'Light, spectra, and the Doppler shift', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how a spectrum splits light into its component wavelengths, tell the difference between continuous, emission, and absorption spectra, and explain how the Doppler effect shifts spectral lines when an object moves toward or away from us. You will be able to define redshift with a simple formula and use it to decide whether a star is approaching or receding. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, recall that light from a receding source is redshifted. Before reading, think about a passing siren: why does its pitch drop as it goes by? The same idea, applied to light instead of sound, is one of the most powerful tools in all of astrophysics, because it turns a faint smear of starlight into a measurement of chemistry, temperature, and motion across billions of light-years. Keep that siren in mind as a mental model for everything that follows."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"When white light passes through a prism or a diffraction grating, it spreads into a rainbow called a spectrum, because each wavelength bends by a different amount. A hot, dense object such as a star''s surface gives a smooth continuous spectrum. A thin hot gas emits light only at certain wavelengths, producing bright emission lines whose pattern is a fingerprint of the elements present. When continuous light passes through cooler gas, that gas absorbs the same special wavelengths, leaving dark absorption lines. By reading these lines, astronomers learn what a distant object is made of without ever touching it. The Doppler effect then adds motion to the picture. If a source moves toward us, its waves are squeezed to shorter wavelengths, a blueshift; if it moves away, the waves are stretched to longer wavelengths, a redshift. Because the line patterns are known from laboratory measurements, any shift in their position directly reveals the speed along our line of sight."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"The redshift z is defined as z = (observed wavelength - rest wavelength) / rest wavelength. For speeds well below the speed of light, z is approximately v / c, where v is the speed along the line of sight and c is the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometers per second. Example: a hydrogen line normally at 656 nanometers is seen at 663 nanometers. Then z = (663 - 656) / 656, which is about 0.011, so v is roughly 0.011 times c, near 3,200 kilometers per second away from us. A positive z means recession; a shift to shorter wavelength would give a negative value and mean approach. This single idea lets astronomers measure the spin of a galaxy, detect a planet tugging its star back and forth, and chart the expansion of the universe. The same spectral lines reveal more: hotter gas broadens the lines, and strong magnetic fields can split a line into closely spaced pairs."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A spectral line at rest is 500 nanometers and is observed at 505 nanometers. Is the source approaching or receding? 2) Compute its redshift z. 3) Estimate its speed as a fraction of c. 4) What kind of spectrum shows dark lines on a bright continuous background? 5) Why can two stars made of the same elements show lines at slightly different wavelengths? Answers: 1) Receding, because the wavelength grew longer (a redshift). 2) z = (505 - 500) / 500 = 5 / 500 = 0.01. 3) v is about 0.01 times c, roughly 3,000 kilometers per second. 4) An absorption spectrum, where cool gas removes specific wavelengths from continuous light. 5) Because they move at different speeds along our line of sight, so the Doppler shift moves their identical line patterns by different amounts."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking redshift means the light literally turns red - it means every wavelength is stretched by the same factor, so all lines move toward the longer-wavelength end whatever their starting color. Mistake: confusing the three spectra; remember that a dense hot surface gives a continuous rainbow, a thin hot gas gives bright emission lines, and cool gas in front of a hot source gives dark absorption lines. Mistake: using z is about v / c for objects moving near the speed of light, where the simple formula breaks down and a relativistic version is needed. Exit ticket: (a) Define redshift in words. (b) If a galaxy''s lines are shifted to longer wavelengths, is it moving toward or away from us? Answers: (a) the fractional increase in wavelength, equal to (observed minus rest) divided by rest; (b) away from us."}]}'::jsonb, 'Blueshift means wavelengths get shorter (motion toward us); redshift means wavelengths get longer (motion away). The color names are a convention, not literally blue or red.', 13),
  (4, 'Stars: formation, the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram, and fusion', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how a star forms from a collapsing cloud of gas, read the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram that organizes stars by temperature and brightness, and describe how nuclear fusion powers a star for most of its life. You will be able to say why more massive stars are hotter, brighter, and shorter-lived than lighter ones. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, recall that stars shine by nuclear fusion, not by burning like a fire. Before reading, ask yourself where the energy of sunlight ultimately comes from, and why the Sun has kept shining steadily for about 4.6 billion years without running out. The answer lies in the crushing pressure and temperature at a star''s core, where hydrogen is converted into helium and a tiny fraction of mass becomes an enormous amount of energy that streams outward as light."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Stars are born inside cold, dense clouds of gas and dust. When part of a cloud becomes dense enough, gravity pulls it inward, and as it collapses the center heats up. If the core reaches about ten million kelvin, hydrogen nuclei begin to fuse into helium, and a star is born. The outward push of this energy balances the inward pull of gravity, a steady standoff called hydrostatic equilibrium that can last billions of years. Astronomers summarize the whole family of stars on the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram, which plots brightness (luminosity) against surface temperature. Most stars, including the Sun, fall along a sweeping band called the main sequence, where hydrogen fusion is steady. A star''s position on that band is set almost entirely by its mass: heavier stars are hotter, far brighter, and bluer, while lighter stars are cooler, dimmer, and redder. Puffed-up giants and tiny white dwarfs sit off the main band."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"The energy of fusion comes from Einstein''s relation E = m times c^2, where c is the speed of light. In the Sun, four hydrogen nuclei fuse into one helium nucleus, and the helium weighs slightly less than the four hydrogens; that missing mass, under one percent, becomes energy. Because c^2 is a huge number, even a tiny lost mass releases tremendous energy, which is why the Sun converts millions of tonnes of mass into light every second yet barely changes over billions of years. Mass also sets lifetime in a surprising way: a star ten times the Sun''s mass has far more fuel, but it burns that fuel so much faster that it lives only a few million years instead of ten billion. Hot, massive blue stars on the upper left of the main sequence are therefore rare and short-lived, while cool, low-mass red dwarfs on the lower right can shine steadily for far longer than the current age of the universe."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What triggers a cloud of gas to begin collapsing into a star? 2) Roughly what core temperature is needed to start hydrogen fusion? 3) On the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram, where do hot blue stars sit, left or right? 4) Does a more massive main-sequence star live longer or shorter than the Sun, and why? 5) In plain words, what does E = m times c^2 tell us about fusion? Answers: 1) Gravity, once a region is dense enough to overcome the gas pressure holding it up. 2) About ten million kelvin. 3) On the left, because the temperature axis increases to the left. 4) Shorter, because it burns through its larger fuel supply far faster. 5) A small amount of mass lost when nuclei fuse is converted into a large amount of energy, since the speed of light squared is an enormous number."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: picturing a star as a ball of fire that burns chemically; stars release energy by nuclear fusion of light elements, which is millions of times more powerful than any chemical burning. Mistake: reading the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram with temperature increasing to the right - by long-standing convention it increases to the left, so hot blue stars are on the left. Mistake: assuming bigger stars live longer because they hold more fuel; they actually live shorter lives because their higher core temperatures burn that fuel at a runaway pace. Exit ticket: (a) Name the steady balance between gravity and fusion pressure that keeps a star stable. (b) What single property of a star most determines its brightness, color, and lifetime? Answers: (a) hydrostatic equilibrium; (b) its mass."}]}'::jsonb, 'On the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram the temperature axis runs backward: hot blue stars sit on the left, cool red stars on the right.', 14),
  (5, 'Stellar death: white dwarfs, neutron stars, supernovae, and black holes', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe what happens when a star exhausts its nuclear fuel, and match a star''s mass to its final state: a white dwarf, a neutron star, or a black hole. You will be able to explain what a supernova is and why the heavy elements in your own body were forged in dying stars. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, recall that a black hole is a region whose gravity is so strong that not even light escapes. Before reading, note that the Sun will not explode; most stars end quietly. The dramatic deaths - the supernovae that briefly outshine whole galaxies - are reserved for the rare heavy stars. Understanding these endings explains where elements such as carbon, oxygen, and iron come from, and how the densest objects in the universe are made from collapsed stellar cores."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"When a star uses up the hydrogen in its core, fusion can no longer hold back gravity, and the star changes dramatically. A Sun-like star swells into a red giant, fuses helium for a while, then gently sheds its outer layers as a glowing shell and leaves behind a hot, dense core called a white dwarf, about the size of Earth but as heavy as the Sun. A white dwarf no longer fuses anything; it simply cools over billions of years. Much heavier stars go further, fusing elements all the way up to iron. Iron cannot release energy by fusing, so the core suddenly collapses and the outer layers crash inward and rebound in a titanic explosion called a supernova. What the leftover core becomes depends on its mass: a moderately heavy core forms a neutron star, while the heaviest cores collapse without limit into a black hole."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"A white dwarf is held up not by heat but by a quantum effect called electron degeneracy pressure, and it cannot exceed about 1.4 times the Sun''s mass, a ceiling named the Chandrasekhar limit. A neutron star packs more than the Sun''s mass into a sphere only about twenty kilometers across, so dense that a sugar-cube of its matter would weigh as much as a mountain; many spin rapidly and sweep beams of radio waves past Earth as pulsars. A black hole forms when gravity overwhelms every form of pressure. Its boundary, the event horizon, marks where the escape speed equals the speed of light, so nothing inside can get out; the horizon radius grows in direct proportion to the mass. Supernovae matter for life itself: the explosion forges and scatters heavy elements such as oxygen, calcium, and iron into space, where they later gather into new stars and planets. The iron in your blood was made inside an ancient star."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is the final state of a star about the mass of the Sun? 2) Why can a white dwarf not grow heavier than about 1.4 solar masses? 3) What is a pulsar? 4) At the event horizon of a black hole, what does the escape speed equal? 5) Why are supernovae important for the existence of planets and life? Answers: 1) A white dwarf, left behind after the star sheds its outer layers. 2) Above the Chandrasekhar limit of about 1.4 solar masses, electron degeneracy pressure can no longer support it. 3) A rapidly spinning neutron star whose radio beams sweep past us like a lighthouse, giving regular pulses. 4) The speed of light, so not even light can escape from within. 5) They forge heavy elements and scatter them into space, providing the raw material for rocky planets and living things."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming every star ends in a supernova or a black hole; in fact the great majority, including the Sun, end quietly as white dwarfs. Mistake: thinking a black hole sucks in everything nearby like a vacuum cleaner - far from the horizon its gravity is the same as that of any object of equal mass, so a planet at a safe distance would simply orbit it. Mistake: believing the heavy elements were present from the very start of the universe; most were built inside stars and spread by their deaths. Exit ticket: (a) Order these remnants by the mass of the collapsing core, lightest first: black hole, white dwarf, neutron star. (b) What is the name for the boundary of a black hole? Answers: (a) white dwarf, neutron star, black hole; (b) the event horizon."}]}'::jsonb, 'The mass that decides a star''s fate is the mass of its core at the end, not the mass it was born with, because stars shed outer layers as they age.', 14),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which of these has the longest wavelength?","options":["radio waves","gamma rays","X-rays","ultraviolet light"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Radio sits at the long-wavelength end of the electromagnetic spectrum."},{"prompt":"A spectrum showing dark lines at specific wavelengths is a(n)...","options":["continuous spectrum","absorption spectrum","spectrum carrying no information","blueshifted rainbow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cool gas in front of a hot source removes specific wavelengths, leaving dark absorption lines."},{"prompt":"For speeds well below light speed, the redshift z is approximately...","options":["v / c","c / v","v times c","c squared"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The small-speed Doppler formula gives z about equal to v / c."},{"prompt":"On the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram, most stars lie along the...","options":["white dwarf cooling track","main sequence","event horizon","habitable zone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The main sequence is the band of stars steadily fusing hydrogen into helium."},{"prompt":"A star about the mass of the Sun ends its life as a...","options":["black hole","neutron star","white dwarf","quasar"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A Sun-like star sheds its outer layers and leaves a cooling white dwarf."},{"prompt":"A supernova occurs when...","options":["a planet forms from dust","a massive star''s core collapses","two comets collide","a sunspot appears"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When a massive star''s iron core collapses, the rebound blows the star apart as a supernova."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (7, 'Galaxies, the Milky Way, and the interstellar medium', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe what a galaxy is, locate the Sun within the Milky Way, name the main galaxy shapes, and explain what the interstellar medium is and why star-forming regions glow. You will be able to say roughly how many stars the Milky Way holds and what sits at its center. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, recall that the Sun lives in the Milky Way galaxy. Before reading, try to picture the scale: the Milky Way is so large that light, the fastest thing there is, takes about 100,000 years just to cross it. Galaxies are the basic building blocks of the large-scale universe, and the space between their stars is not empty but filled with thin gas and dust that forms the next generation of suns."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A galaxy is an enormous, gravitationally bound system of stars, gas, dust, and dark matter. The Milky Way is our home galaxy, a barred spiral holding a few hundred billion stars arranged in a flat disk with curving arms, a central bulge, and a surrounding halo. The Sun sits in the disk, roughly two thirds of the way out from the center, and takes about 230 million years to orbit once. At the very center lies a supermassive black hole, several million times the Sun''s mass, whose presence is revealed by the fast orbits of stars around an invisible point. Galaxies come in three broad shapes: spirals with arms and ongoing star formation, ellipticals that are rounded and made mostly of old stars, and irregulars with no clear structure. Between the stars lies the interstellar medium, a thin mixture of gas and dust that is the reservoir from which new stars are born."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"The interstellar medium is extraordinarily thin - on average far emptier than any laboratory vacuum - yet because space is so vast, it adds up to a huge mass of raw material. Where this gas gathers into dense, cold clouds, gravity can pull it together into new stars. A glowing cloud of this kind is called a nebula. The Orion Nebula, visible to the unaided eye as a fuzzy patch in Orion''s sword, is a nearby stellar nursery where hundreds of young stars are forming right now. There are three broad kinds of nebula: emission nebulae, where ultraviolet light from hot young stars makes the surrounding hydrogen glow red; reflection nebulae, where dust scatters starlight and shines blue; and dark nebulae, where thick dust simply blocks the light behind it. Dust also reddens and dims starlight passing through it, much as smoke reddens a sunset, an effect astronomers must correct for when they measure distances and brightnesses across the galaxy."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What type of galaxy is the Milky Way? 2) Roughly how long does light take to cross the Milky Way? 3) What sits at the center of our galaxy? 4) What is the interstellar medium made of? 5) Why does an emission nebula glow? Answers: 1) A barred spiral galaxy. 2) About 100,000 years, since it is roughly 100,000 light-years across. 3) A supermassive black hole, several million times the mass of the Sun. 4) Thin gas, mostly hydrogen, and dust spread between the stars. 5) Ultraviolet light from hot, young nearby stars energizes the hydrogen gas, which then re-emits visible light and glows red."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining the space between stars is a perfect vacuum; it holds a thin but important medium of gas and dust that forms future stars. Mistake: thinking the Sun sits at the center of the Milky Way - it actually orbits far out in the disk. Mistake: confusing a galaxy with the whole universe; the Milky Way is just one of roughly two trillion galaxies we can observe. Mistake: treating a light-year as a measure of time rather than distance. Exit ticket: (a) Name the three broad shapes of galaxies. (b) What do we call a cloud of interstellar gas and dust, especially one lit up near young stars? Answers: (a) spiral, elliptical, and irregular; (b) a nebula."}]}'::jsonb, 'A light-year is a distance, not a time: it is how far light travels in one year, about 9.5 trillion kilometers.', 13),
  (8, 'Cosmology: Hubble''s law, the expanding universe, dark matter, and dark energy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State Hubble''s law, explain what it means for the universe to be expanding, outline the Big Bang picture and its main evidence, and describe why astronomers infer dark matter and dark energy. You will be able to connect a galaxy''s redshift to its distance. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, recall that more distant galaxies recede faster. Before reading, imagine dots drawn on a balloon that is being inflated: every dot sees all the others moving away, and the farther ones move away faster, yet no dot is the center. That simple picture captures the expanding universe, one of the great discoveries of twentieth-century science, and it leads directly to the idea that the cosmos had a hot, dense beginning we call the Big Bang."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In the 1920s Edwin Hubble found that nearly every galaxy is receding from us, and that the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it recedes. This relation is Hubble''s law, written v = H0 times d, where v is the recession speed, d is the distance, and H0 is the Hubble constant. The natural explanation is that space itself is expanding, carrying galaxies apart like raisins in rising dough. Running the expansion backward in time means everything was once packed together, hot and dense - the Big Bang, about 13.8 billion years ago. This was not an explosion in space but an expansion of space. The strongest evidence is the cosmic microwave background, a faint glow of radiation left over from when the young universe first cooled enough to become transparent, now stretched to microwave wavelengths and reaching us from every direction. The measured proportions of hydrogen and helium also match what the hot early universe should have produced."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"Hubble''s law doubles as a distance tool: measure a galaxy''s redshift, convert it to a recession speed, and divide by H0 to estimate its distance. Two deeper puzzles then appear. First, galaxies rotate too fast and clusters hold together too tightly for the gravity of their visible matter alone; something unseen must supply the extra gravity. This is dark matter, which neither emits nor absorbs light and outweighs ordinary matter by about five to one. Second, in the late 1990s astronomers studying distant supernovae found that the expansion of the universe is not slowing down under gravity but speeding up. The agent driving this acceleration is called dark energy. Putting the budget together, ordinary matter - everything we can see - is only about five percent of the universe, dark matter about twenty-seven percent, and dark energy about sixty-eight percent. In other words, the familiar stuff of stars and planets is a thin layer on a mostly dark cosmos."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write Hubble''s law and say what each symbol means. 2) If galaxy A is twice as far as galaxy B, how do their recession speeds compare? 3) What leftover radiation is the key evidence for the Big Bang? 4) Why do astronomers think dark matter exists? 5) What did distant supernovae reveal about the expansion? Answers: 1) v = H0 times d, where v is recession speed, d is distance, and H0 is the Hubble constant. 2) Galaxy A recedes about twice as fast, since speed is proportional to distance. 3) The cosmic microwave background. 4) Galaxies and clusters show more gravity than their visible matter can provide, implying unseen mass. 5) That the expansion is accelerating, which points to dark energy."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: picturing the Big Bang as an explosion that threw matter outward from one spot in pre-existing space; it was space itself beginning to expand everywhere at once, with no center and no edge inside the universe. Mistake: thinking galaxies move away because they are flying through space; mostly the space between them is stretching. Mistake: treating dark matter and dark energy as the same thing - dark matter adds gravity and holds structures together, while dark energy pushes the expansion to accelerate. Exit ticket: (a) What does Hubble''s law relate? (b) Which two unseen components make up most of the universe, and which one speeds up the expansion? Answers: (a) a galaxy''s recession speed and its distance, with speed proportional to distance; (b) dark matter and dark energy, with dark energy driving the accelerating expansion."}]}'::jsonb, 'Expansion stretches the space between galaxies; the galaxies are not flying out from a central point, so there is no single place that counts as the center of the expansion.', 14),
  (9, 'Exoplanets, the search for life, and gravitational waves', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the main methods used to discover planets around other stars, describe what the habitable zone is, and outline how gravitational waves were detected and why they opened a new window on the universe. You will be able to name what astronomers look for as possible signs of life. Warm-up: think about how you could detect a planet you cannot see directly, around a star trillions of kilometers away. Two clever tricks - watching a star dim slightly and watching it wobble - have together revealed thousands of worlds. Meanwhile, a century after they were predicted, ripples in spacetime itself were finally measured, confirming a bold prediction and letting us listen to colliding black holes. This lesson surveys some of the most active frontiers of modern astrophysics."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An exoplanet is a planet that orbits a star other than the Sun. The first ones around ordinary stars were confirmed in the 1990s, and today thousands are known. Two methods dominate. In the transit method, a planet passing in front of its star blocks a tiny fraction of the starlight, causing a small, regular dip in brightness; the depth of the dip reveals the planet''s size, and its timing reveals the orbit. In the radial-velocity method, the planet''s gravity tugs the star in a small circle, and that motion shows up as a back-and-forth Doppler shift in the star''s spectrum, revealing the planet''s mass. A key goal is to find planets in the habitable zone, the band of orbital distances where temperatures allow liquid water on a surface. Separately, Einstein''s general theory of relativity predicted gravitational waves: ripples in the fabric of spacetime produced when massive objects accelerate violently, such as two black holes spiraling together."},{"heading":"Key ideas & examples","text":"The transit method was used by the Kepler space telescope to find thousands of planets, including small rocky worlds not much larger than Earth. By combining a transit size with a radial-velocity mass, astronomers can estimate a planet''s density and guess whether it is rocky or gaseous. When a planet transits, a little starlight filters through its atmosphere, imprinting absorption lines that reveal the gases present; this is how observers search for water vapor and other molecules. The search for life looks for biosignatures - combinations of gases, such as oxygen together with methane, that would be hard to maintain without living processes. Gravitational waves arrived as direct evidence in 2015, when the LIGO detectors measured the spacetime ripple from two black holes merging more than a billion light-years away. The signal stretched and squeezed the detectors'' four-kilometer arms by less than the width of a proton, yet it matched the prediction precisely, and later detections even caught two neutron stars merging, seen in both gravitational waves and light."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In the transit method, what do we observe when a planet crosses in front of its star? 2) What does the radial-velocity method measure about a planet? 3) What is the habitable zone? 4) Give one example of a possible biosignature. 5) What are gravitational waves, and name the instrument that first detected them. Answers: 1) A small, regular dip in the star''s brightness as the planet blocks part of its light. 2) The planet''s mass, from the Doppler wobble it causes in the star. 3) The range of distances from a star where a planet could have liquid water on its surface. 4) Oxygen together with methane, a combination hard to sustain without life. 5) Ripples in spacetime caused by violently accelerating masses, first detected directly by LIGO in 2015."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining astronomers usually photograph exoplanets directly; most are found indirectly, by the dimming of a transit or the wobble of radial velocity, because the planets are tiny and lost in their star''s glare. Mistake: assuming any planet in the habitable zone must have life; the zone only allows the possibility of liquid water, which is necessary but far from sufficient. Mistake: thinking gravitational waves are waves of light or sound; they are a stretching and squeezing of space itself, utterly different from electromagnetic waves, which is why they carry new information. Exit ticket: (a) Name the two main methods for discovering exoplanets. (b) What did the 2015 LIGO detection confirm? Answers: (a) the transit method and the radial-velocity method; (b) that gravitational waves exist, produced here by two merging black holes, confirming a prediction of general relativity."}]}'::jsonb, 'An exoplanet is simply a planet orbiting a star other than the Sun. The habitable zone is the range of distances where a planet could hold liquid water on its surface.', 13),
  (10, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"luminosity","translation":"the total amount of energy a star radiates each second, its true brightness regardless of distance","example":"A blue giant can have a luminosity tens of thousands of times that of the Sun."},{"term":"light-year","translation":"the distance light travels in one year, about 9.5 trillion kilometers","example":"The nearest star beyond the Sun is a little over four light-years away."},{"term":"redshift","translation":"the stretching of light to longer wavelengths when a source recedes or when space expands","example":"The galaxy''s large redshift showed it was receding from us quickly."},{"term":"main sequence","translation":"the band on the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram where stars steadily fuse hydrogen into helium","example":"The Sun will spend about ten billion years on the main sequence."},{"term":"supernova","translation":"the explosive death of a massive star, which can briefly outshine an entire galaxy","example":"A supernova scatters heavy elements into space to seed future planets."},{"term":"event horizon","translation":"the boundary of a black hole, beyond which not even light can escape","example":"Once past the event horizon, no signal can ever reach us again."},{"term":"nebula","translation":"a cloud of gas and dust in space, often a region where new stars are forming","example":"The Orion Nebula is a nearby nursery of young stars."},{"term":"cosmic microwave background","translation":"the faint leftover radiation from the hot early universe, now stretched to microwave wavelengths","example":"The cosmic microwave background is a cornerstone of evidence for the Big Bang."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d' and l.position = v.pos
);
